<!-- ============================================================
     Mistic Backdoor / KongTuke Access Broker Detection Rules
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK Techniques:
       T1566.002 - Phishing: Spearphishing Link (Teams ClickFix delivery)
       T1574.002 - Hijack Execution Flow: DLL Side-Loading
       T1055    - Process Injection (in-memory BOF execution)
       T1056.004 - Input Capture: Credential API Hooking / Fake UI
       T1071.001 - Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols (C2)
       T1140    - Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information
       T1070.004 - File Deletion (self-deletion / kill switch)
     Rule IDs: 103590-103599
     Author: SecOps Engineer
     Date: 2026-06-24

     Threat actor summary:
       KongTuke is a financially-motivated access broker targeting insurance,
       education, IT, and professional services. It deploys Mistic (also called
       MTLBackdoor) as a stealthy implant to establish persistent access for
       ransomware affiliates. First reported publicly June 24, 2026.

     Attack chain:
       1. Social engineering via Microsoft Teams (fake IT/HR notifications)
       2. ClickFix lure delivers multi-stage infection:
          - ModeloRAT deployed first as initial implant
          - Mistic deployed as follow-on persistent backdoor
       3. DLL side-loading: legitimate MpExtMs.exe loads malicious version.dll
          which loads EndpointDlp.dll (Mistic core, mimics MDATP naming)
       4. .NET DLL presents fake credential/MFA prompt (T1056.004)
       5. Mistic runs payloads in memory via BOF with no disk artifacts
       6. Self-deletion kill switch removes all trace on operator command

     Key IOCs (at time of publication  - no specific C2 domains/IPs disclosed):
       - MpExtMs.exe loading version.dll from non-standard path
       - EndpointDlp.dll loaded by MpExtMs.exe from %TEMP% or user-writable dirs
       - .NET DLL spawning fake UI credential prompt
       - DLL loaded by MpExtMs.exe performing outbound HTTP/HTTPS connections

     Detection approach:
       1. MpExtMs.exe loading version.dll from non-Program Files path (sideload)
       2. EndpointDlp.dll created/accessed outside MDATP install directory
       3. MpExtMs.exe making outbound network connections (atypical for this binary)
       4. .NET executable spawning GUI window presenting credential prompts
       5. Fake Teams notification leading to CLI command execution (ClickFix anchor)
       6. File self-deletion immediately after in-memory DLL execution
       7. MpExtMs.exe child process creation (atypical for Microsoft binary)
       8. In-memory BOF execution indicators (unsigned code in process memory)
       9. Correlated DLL sideload + outbound C2 connection chain

     Severity tiers:
       Low (5)      - Base anchor (correlation only)
       Medium (9)   - Suspicious but context-dependent; investigate
       High (11-12) - Strong KongTuke/Mistic indicator; investigate promptly
       Critical (13-15) - Near-certain Mistic implant; immediate incident response

     Log source prerequisites:
       - Windows Sysmon (EventID 1/3/7/11) forwarded to Wazuh via NXLog/Winlogbeat
       - Windows Security Event Log (4688, 4663, 4624) via Wazuh Windows agent
       - Wazuh FIM enabled for %TEMP%, %APPDATA%, C:\Windows\Temp
       - Sysmon Event 7 (ImageLoad) requires Sysmon configured with DLL load logging
       - Sysmon Event 3 (NetworkConnect) enabled for all processes

     References:
       - BleepingComputer: "Stealthy Mistic Backdoor Linked to Ransomware Access
         Broker KongTuke" (2026-06-24)
       - MITRE ATT&amp;CK: T1574.002 (DLL Side-Loading)
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="windows,kongtuke,mistic,malware,defense_evasion,credential_access,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1574.002  - DLL side-loading anchor: MpExtMs.exe base event
       Rule IDs: 103590
       MpExtMs.exe is a legitimate Microsoft Defender for Endpoint
       helper binary. KongTuke places a malicious version.dll in the
       same directory and runs MpExtMs.exe to trigger the sideload.
       Any execution of MpExtMs.exe outside of MDATP/MsSense install
       paths is suspicious.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="103590" level="9">
    <if_group>windows,sysmon</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)MpExtMs\.exe</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(C:\\Program Files\\Windows Defender|C:\\ProgramData\\Microsoft\\Windows Defender|C:\\Program Files\\Microsoft Security Client)</field>
    <description>Mistic/KongTuke: MpExtMs.exe executed from non-standard path on $(hostname)  -  DLL side-loading precursor; MpExtMs.exe should only run from Windows Defender install directory (T1574.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1574.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>mistic_dll_sideload,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1574.002  - version.dll loaded by MpExtMs.exe from wrong path
       Rule IDs: 103591
       Sysmon Event 7 (ImageLoad): MpExtMs.exe loading version.dll
       from any path outside System32 is the core DLL sideload signal.
       version.dll is a legitimate Windows DLL; the malicious copy
       is planted in the same directory as MpExtMs.exe.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="103591" level="14">
    <if_group>windows,sysmon</if_group>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">7</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)MpExtMs\.exe</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.imageLoaded" type="pcre2">(?i)version\.dll</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.imageLoaded" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(C:\\Windows\\System32\\version\.dll|C:\\Windows\\SysWOW64\\version\.dll)</field>
    <description>Mistic/KongTuke DLL sideload confirmed on $(hostname): MpExtMs.exe loaded version.dll from non-System32 path  -  matches KongTuke Mistic implant delivery technique (T1574.002); immediate investigation required</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1574.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>mistic_dll_sideload,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1574.002  - EndpointDlp.dll created/accessed outside MDATP
       Rule IDs: 103592
       EndpointDlp.dll is the filename used by Mistic core implant,
       chosen to mimic Microsoft Defender for Endpoint's legitimate
       endpoint DLP component. Any creation or load of EndpointDlp.dll
       outside the official MDATP install path is malicious.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="103592" level="13">
    <if_group>windows,sysmon</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename|win.eventdata.imageLoaded" type="pcre2">(?i)EndpointDlp\.dll</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename|win.eventdata.imageLoaded" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(C:\\Program Files\\Windows Defender|C:\\ProgramData\\Microsoft\\Windows Defender Advanced Threat Protection)</field>
    <description>Mistic backdoor artifact on $(hostname): EndpointDlp.dll found/loaded outside MDATP directory  -  Mistic uses this filename to masquerade as Microsoft DLP component (T1574.002, T1036.005)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1574.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>mistic_endpoint_dlp,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1071.001  - MpExtMs.exe outbound network connection
       Rule IDs: 103593
       MpExtMs.exe is a Microsoft Defender helper binary that does not
       initiate outbound network connections in normal operation. Any
       network connection from this process indicates Mistic is
       communicating with C2 infrastructure via the sideloaded DLL.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="103593" level="14">
    <if_group>windows,sysmon</if_group>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">3</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)MpExtMs\.exe</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.destinationPort" negate="yes" type="pcre2">^(135|445|389|636|88|53)$</field>
    <description>Mistic/KongTuke C2 communication on $(hostname): MpExtMs.exe initiated outbound network connection  -  this binary should not make network connections; Mistic is beaconing via sideloaded DLL (T1574.002, T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1574.002</id>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>mistic_c2_connection,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1574.002 + T1071.001  - Composite: sideload confirmed + C2
       Rule IDs: 103594
       High-fidelity correlation: DLL sideload event (103591/103592)
       followed by outbound connection from MpExtMs.exe (103593)
       within 5 minutes is a near-certain Mistic implant active session.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="103594" level="15" frequency="1" timeframe="300">
    <if_matched_sid>103591</if_matched_sid>
    <if_matched_group>mistic_c2_connection,</if_matched_group>
    <description>MISTIC IMPLANT ACTIVE on $(hostname): DLL sideload via MpExtMs.exe/version.dll confirmed + outbound C2 connection from MpExtMs.exe within 5 minutes  -  KongTuke Mistic backdoor fully operational; CRITICAL incident response required (T1574.002, T1071.001)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1574.002</id>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>mistic_active_implant,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1056.004  - Fake credential prompt (.NET UI DLL)
       Rule IDs: 103595
       KongTuke deploys a .NET DLL alongside Mistic that renders a
       fake MFA or login window to harvest credentials. Detection:
       .NET executable presenting a credential window with parent
       being MpExtMs.exe or a suspicious loader.
       Sysmon Event 1: process creation with parent MpExtMs.exe
       spawning a .NET executable with UI characteristics.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="103595" level="12">
    <if_group>windows,sysmon</if_group>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">1</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)MpExtMs\.exe</field>
    <description>Mistic/KongTuke fake credential UI on $(hostname): process created with MpExtMs.exe as parent  -  KongTuke deploys .NET credential harvesting DLL loaded by MpExtMs.exe; child process creation from this binary is anomalous (T1056.004, T1574.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1056.004</id>
      <id>T1574.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>mistic_credential_harvest,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1566.002 / T1204.001  - ClickFix Teams delivery anchor
       Rule IDs: 103596
       KongTuke delivers Mistic via Teams ClickFix social engineering.
       The lure instructs the victim to copy-paste a PowerShell or
       cmd command into a terminal. Detection: Teams process spawning
       shell or executing mshta/wscript with Base64 encoded command.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="103596" level="11">
    <if_group>windows,sysmon</if_group>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">1</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)(Teams\.exe|msedge\.exe|chrome\.exe|msedgewebview2\.exe)</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)(powershell\.exe|pwsh\.exe|cmd\.exe|mshta\.exe|wscript\.exe|cscript\.exe|rundll32\.exe)</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(-enc\s+[A-Za-z0-9+/=]{20,}|-EncodedCommand|FromBase64String|[A-Za-z0-9+/]{50,}={0,2})</field>
    <description>ClickFix lure execution on $(hostname): Teams/browser spawned PowerShell/cmd with Base64-encoded command  -  KongTuke Teams ClickFix delivery pattern; Mistic implant deployment likely in progress (T1566.002, T1204.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1566.002</id>
      <id>T1204.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>kongtuke_clickfix,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1070.004  - Mistic self-deletion (kill switch artifact)
       Rule IDs: 103597
       Mistic includes a self-deletion kill switch: on operator
       command, it deletes itself and its loader DLLs.
       Detection: cmd.exe spawned by MpExtMs.exe using del or
       ping-then-delete self-deletion pattern (cmd /c ping -n 3
       127.0.0.1 &amp; del [path]).
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="103597" level="13">
    <if_group>windows,sysmon</if_group>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">1</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)MpExtMs\.exe</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)(cmd\.exe|powershell\.exe)</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(del\s+.*\.(dll|exe)|remove-item.*\.(dll|exe)|ping.*&amp;.*del|timeout.*&amp;.*del|erase\s+.*\.(dll|exe))</field>
    <description>Mistic self-deletion on $(hostname): MpExtMs.exe spawned cmd/PowerShell with file deletion command  -  Mistic kill switch triggered; implant attempting to remove itself; capture memory image immediately (T1070.004, T1574.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1070.004</id>
      <id>T1574.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>mistic_self_delete,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1574.002  - Unsigned/untrusted DLL loaded by MpExtMs.exe
       Rule IDs: 103598
       Sysmon Event 7 with Signature=false or invalid signature on
       any DLL loaded by MpExtMs.exe. The malicious version.dll
       and EndpointDlp.dll will not have valid Microsoft signatures.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="103598" level="12">
    <if_group>windows,sysmon</if_group>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">7</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)MpExtMs\.exe</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.signed" type="pcre2">(?i)^(false|no|0)$</field>
    <description>Mistic/KongTuke unsigned DLL loaded on $(hostname): MpExtMs.exe loaded an unsigned DLL  -  malicious sideload DLL (version.dll or EndpointDlp.dll) identified; investigate DLL path and file hash (T1574.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1574.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>mistic_unsigned_dll,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1574.002  - General KongTuke/Mistic filename IOC sweep
       Rule IDs: 103599
       FIM/Sysmon file create events for the known Mistic filenames
       in any user-writable location. Cast wide  - covers cases where
       the binary name is used as a staging artifact.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="103599" level="11">
    <if_group>windows,sysmon</if_group>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">11</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)(EndpointDlp\.dll|MpExtMs\.exe|MTLBackdoor)</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(C:\\Program Files\\Windows Defender|C:\\ProgramData\\Microsoft\\Windows Defender Advanced Threat Protection)</field>
    <description>Mistic/KongTuke file artifact on $(hostname): known Mistic implant filename (EndpointDlp.dll / MpExtMs.exe outside MDATP / MTLBackdoor) written to disk  -  active staging; collect file sample and investigate (T1574.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1574.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>mistic_file_artifact,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
